+# EXL3 recipes for SenseNova-Loop-9B
+
+One file per tier. Each names the three widths `exllamav3`'s `convert_model.py` takes for that run; the
+layout is the same in every one (see [../exl3-sensenova-quantize.md](../exl3-sensenova-quantize.md)):
+
+| key | flag | meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `bits` | `-b` | body (every quantized linear but the head) |
+| `head_bits` | `-hb` | `lm_head` |
+| `embed_bits` | `-eb` | `embed_tokens`; 16 keeps it BF16, which the Mac reads from the mapped file |
+| `codebook` | `-cb` | `mul1`: CUDA admits only this, and it carries the half-bit widths |
+| `out_scales` | `--out_scales` | `always`: a Hessian-only run collects no output statistics for `auto` to decide with |
+
+| recipe | tier | body | head | note |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `4.0` | Mac default, RTX 16 GB small | 4.0 | 6 | about 6.3 GB with the BF16 embedding |
+| `5.0` | Mac quality, RTX 16 GB default | 5.0 | 6 | about 7.0 GB with the BF16 embedding |
+| `6.0` | RTX 16 GB quality | 6.0 | 8 | about 8.1 GB with the BF16 embedding |
+
+One pack per recipe serves both runners. The Mac reads the embedding from the mapped file, so its GPU-resident
+weights are the body and head alone; the RTX tiers keep the embedding on the card. The Mac numbers and their
+gate are in the Metal plan (`docs/exl3-quant-plan.md`); the RTX caps are in the EXL3 spec.
+[`tools/exl3_sensenova_chain.sh`](../../tools/exl3_sensenova_chain.sh) converts every file here, in name order.
+
+Credit: EXL3 is [exllamav3](https://github.com/turboderp-org/exllamav3)'s format and quantizer (turboderp);
+its trellis codebooks are QTIP's (Tseng, Chee, Sun, Kuleshov, De Sa, [arXiv:2406.11235](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11235)).
